Count of Smaller Numbers After Self
✅ Problem Statement

Given an integer array nums, return an array counts such that:

counts[i] = number of smaller elements to the right of nums[i]

🧩 Example
Input:
nums = [5, 2, 6, 1]

Output:
[2, 1, 1, 0]

Why?

For each element:

Element	Smaller on Right	Count
5	2,1	2
2	1	1
6	1	1
1	—	0
❌ Brute Force

For every element:

Check all right elements

Time:

O(N²)


Too slow for large N.

🧠 Optimized Solution (INTERVIEW LEVEL)

We use:

✅ Modified Merge Sort

Why?

Because:
During merging,
When right element < left element →
That means it is a smaller number coming after.

We count it.

Time Complexity:

O(N log N)

🔥 Core Idea

Instead of sorting plain numbers, we sort:

[value, originalIndex]


Because:
After sorting, we must update answer at correct index.

🪜 Algorithm Steps

Create array of pairs: (value, index)

Create count[] array

Use merge sort

During merge:

Maintain rightCounter

If rightValue < leftValue → increment counter

Add counter to left’s count

☕ Java Implementation
🔹 Step 1: Pair Class
class Pair {
    int value;
    int index;

    Pair(int v, int i) {
        value = v;
        index = i;
    }
}

🔹 Step 2: Main Function
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public List<Integer> countSmaller(int[] nums) {

        int n = nums.length;

        Pair[] arr = new Pair[n];
        int[] count = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i] = new Pair(nums[i], i);
        }

        mergeSort(arr, 0, n - 1, count);

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int c : count) {
            result.add(c);
        }

        return result;
    }

Line-by-Line Explanation
Pair[] arr


Store value + original index.

int[] count


Stores final answer.

mergeSort(...)


Apply divide & conquer.

🔹 Step 3: Merge Sort
    private void mergeSort(Pair[] arr, int left, int right, int[] count) {

        if (left >= right) return;

        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        mergeSort(arr, left, mid, count);
        mergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right, count);

        merge(arr, left, mid, right, count);
    }


Explanation:

Standard merge sort recursion.

🔹 Step 4: Merge Function (Important)
    private void merge(Pair[] arr, int left, int mid, int right, int[] count) {

        List<Pair> merged = new ArrayList<>();

        int i = left;
        int j = mid + 1;
        int rightCounter = 0;

        while (i <= mid && j <= right) {

            if (arr[j].value < arr[i].value) {
                rightCounter++;
                merged.add(arr[j]);
                j++;
            } else {
                count[arr[i].index] += rightCounter;
                merged.add(arr[i]);
                i++;
            }
        }

        while (i <= mid) {
            count[arr[i].index] += rightCounter;
            merged.add(arr[i]);
            i++;
        }

        while (j <= right) {
            merged.add(arr[j]);
            j++;
        }

        for (int k = left; k <= right; k++) {
            arr[k] = merged.get(k - left);
        }
    }
}

🔍 Deep Explanation (VERY IMPORTANT)

Let’s focus here:

🔹 rightCounter

Tracks how many right-side elements
are smaller than current left element.

Case 1:
if arr[j] < arr[i]


Means:
Right element is smaller.
So increment rightCounter.

Case 2:

Else:
Left element is smaller or equal.

Then:

count[left.index] += rightCounter


Because we already passed those smaller right elements.

🧩 Dry Run

Input:

[5,2,6,1]


Process:

After sorting:

During merge,
5 encounters 2 and 1 → count = 2
2 encounters 1 → count = 1
6 encounters 1 → count = 1
1 → 0

Final:

[2,1,1,0]

